   "source": "Form 10-K for fiscal year ended December 31, 2025 (filed 2026-02-26, accession 0001944013-26-000095), Consolidated Statements of Cash Flows line 'Capital expenditures': $53.5M for FY2025 ($36.6M FY2024, $39.0M FY2023 in the same statement) \u2014 tagged in EDGAR companyfacts under us-gaap PaymentsForCapitalImprovements, a tag the engine does not read; H1 YTD legs of the same tag: $30.3M for 2025-01-01 to 2025-06-30 (10-Q) and $25.3M for 2026-01-01 to 2026-06-30 (Q2 2026 10-Q, accession 0001944013-26-000186). TTM capex = $53.5M - $30.3M + $25.3M = $48.5M. Corroborated by Crane's Q4 2025 earnings release Adjusted Free Cash Flow table: capital expenditures $53.5M (FY2025) / $36.6M (FY2024) against cash provided by operating activities from continuing operations of $394.8M / $257.8M.",
   "reason": "DATA_GAP:capex \u2014 capex missing for fiscal years 2023, 2024, 2025 under every candidate tag the engine reads, because Crane files capital expenditures only as PaymentsForCapitalImprovements. The engine applies this single value to every missing period, and ttm.py fills its capex legs from the same value so the TTM capex comes out at exactly the filer's filed TTM figure of $48.5M \u2014 the TTM base is the base actually used (see analysis.md). Against the true per-year annual figures the single value overstates FY2023 capex by $9.5M ($48.5M vs $39.0M filed) and FY2024 by $11.9M (vs $36.6M filed) and understates FY2025 by $5.0M (vs $53.5M filed); the net effect on the engine-native normalized (median) base is conservative \u2014 about $3.15/share with the override vs $3.36/share built from the true per-year figures (about -6%) \u2014 and that median base is not the base used for the verdict.",
